00:00:03.259 --> 00:00:06.509
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:06.509 --> 00:00:11.700
Let’s talk about a great recent development
in image translation!

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:17.060
Image translation means that some image goes
in, and it is translated into an analogous

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:18.770
image of a different class.

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:24.150
A good example of this would be when we have
a standing tiger as an input, and we ask the

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:29.529
algorithm to translate this image into the
same tiger lying down.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:35.010
This leads to many amazing applications, for
instance, we can specify a daytime image and

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:38.030
get the same scene during nighttime.

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:44.820
We can go from maps to satellite images, from
video games to reality and more.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:49.940
However, much like many learning algorithms
today, most of these techniques have a key

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:56.380
limitation - they need a lot of training data,
or in other words, these neural networks require

00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:02.040
seeing a ton of images in all of these classes
before they can learn to meaningfully translate

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:03.440
between them.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.660
This is clearly inferior to how humans think,
right?

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:12.990
If I would show you a horse, you could easily
imagine, and some of you could even draw what

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:15.670
it would look like if it were a zebra instead.

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:20.740
As I am sure you have noticed by reading arguments
on many internet forums, humans are pretty

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:22.720
good at generalization.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:28.630
So, how could we possibly develop a learning
technique that can look at very few images,

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:32.200
and obtain knowledge from them that generalizes
well?

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:37.870
Have a look at this crazy new paper from scientists
at NVIDIA that accomplishes exactly that.

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:43.560
In this example, they show an input image
of a golden retriever, and then, we specify

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:50.300
the target classes by showing them a bunch
of different animal breeds, and…look!

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:56.250
In goes your golden, and out comes a pug or
any other dog breed you can think of..

00:01:56.250 --> 00:02:01.940
And now, hold on to your papers, because this
AI doesn’t have access to these target images

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:06.850
and it had only seen them the very first time
as we just gave it to them.

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:11.630
It can do this translation with previously
unseen object classes.

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:14.790
How is this insanity even possible?

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:19.610
This work contains a generative adversarial
network, which assumes that the training set

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:25.220
we give it contains images of different animals,
and what it does during training is practicing

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:28.260
the translation process between these animals.

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:33.560
It also contains a class encoder that creates
a low-dimensional latent space for each of

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:39.190
these classes, which means that it tries compress
these images down to a few features that contain

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:42.440
the essence of these individual dog breeds.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:47.650
Apparently it can learn the essence of these
classes really well because it was able to

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:54.330
convert our image into a pug without ever
seeing a pug other than this one target image.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:59.451
As you can see here, it comes out way ahead
of previous techniques, but of course, if

00:02:59.451 --> 00:03:05.340
we give it a target image that is dramatically
different than anything the AI has seen before,

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:06.540
it may falter.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:11.980
Luckily, you can even try it yourself through
this web demo which works on pets, so make

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:16.560
sure to read the instructions carefully, and,
let the experiments begin!

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:22.319
In fact, due to popular request, let me kick
this off with Lisa, my favorite chihuahua.

00:03:22.319 --> 00:03:29.130
I got many tempting alternatives, but worry
not, in reality, she will stay as is.

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:34.870
I was also curious about trying a non-traditional
head position, and as you see with the results,

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:37.870
this was a much more challenging case for
the AI.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:41.450
The paper also discusses this limitation in
more detail.

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:45.880
You know the saying, two more papers down
the line, and I am sure this will also be

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:46.880
remedied.

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:51.879
I am hoping that you will also try your own
pets and as a Fellow Scholar, you will flood

00:03:51.879 --> 00:03:54.550
the comments section here with your findings.

